  "preamble": "Fronting of the velar stop [k] to the palatal stop [c] before front vowels or [j] is one of the most common coarticulatory changes. It is allophonic in Modern Greek, Turkish and Icelandic, where the palatal realization stays a stop rather than proceeding to an affricate, and it is the usual first stage of velar palatalization chains that end in [tʃ] or [ts]. In Albanian, Proto-Albanian *k before front vowels became q [c] (*kela > qell 'to lead'). In Friulian, Latin c before a became the palatal stop /c/ (casa > cjase /ˈca.ze/ 'house').",
  "phoneticEffects": "Palatalization, Velar Fronting",
